# basecamp/.env.example — the keys a deploy must bind.
#
# `deploy.api.envCheck` is true in frontier.config.js, so `fli deploy` refuses a
# server whose .env.production is missing anything named here. That refusal is
# the point of the file: every key below is one whose ABSENCE is silent.

# Where the audit trail is written. The deploy mounts deploy.db.path at /db,
# and the app root inside the image is /app — so the schema's own default
# (./db/audit/) resolves INSIDE the container and the next swap takes the trail
# with it, with the app working perfectly throughout. `fli check`'s
# log-db-unbound is the rule that says so.
AUDIT_PATH=/db/audit.db

# Secret.data is @encrypted under this key, and core/db.ts refuses a production
# boot on the public dev default. `fli deploy:setup` makes one into your local
# .env.production when a fresh box has none, and puts it in the deploy's
# secrets/ directory rather than the env file (FJS-D1352). That local copy is
# the one a restore needs: a database without its key is unreadable.
ENCRYPTION_KEY=
